{"name":"Mission Trail","hex":"#857a64","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mission","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A set of tasks that fulfills a purpose or duty; an assignment set by an employer, or by oneself.","A important goal or purpose; an objective."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To send on a mission.","To do missionary work, proselytize."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mission"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"El Camino Real (California)","blocks":[{"text":"El Camino Real is a 600-mile (965-kilometer) commemorative route connecting the 21 Spanish missions in California from Mission San Diego de Alcalá in San Diego to Mission San Francisco Solano in Sonoma, along with a number of sub-missions, four presidios, and three pueblos. The route is historically associated with a network of royal roads used by inhabitants of New Spain. The modern commemorative route in the U.S. state of California is named after these roads, and is officially defined in the California Streets and Highways Code to run along various roads from Interstate 5 at the Mexican border to State Route 12 in Sonoma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Road_to_LA_-_El_Camino_Real_Bell_in_Front_of_San_Gabriel_Mission_%285540781815%29.jpg/330px-Road_to_LA_-_El_Camino_Real_Bell_in_Front_of_San_Gabriel_Mission_%285540781815%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Camino_Real_(California)","wikidata":"Q805831"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/El_Camino_1850_Cave_J_Couts.jpg/500px-El_Camino_1850_Cave_J_Couts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/El_Camino_1850_Cave_J_Couts.jpg/500px-El_Camino_1850_Cave_J_Couts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"El Camino Real and Couts Path, drawn in 1850 by Cave J. Couts.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEl_Camino_1850_Cave_J_Couts.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/El_Camino_Real_California_2.JPG/500px-El_Camino_Real_California_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/El_Camino_Real_California_2.JPG/1280px-El_Ca